<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oromo</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Oromo are a Cushitic-speaking people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horn-of-africa">Horn of Africa</a>, today one of the largest ethnic groups in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. From the mid-sixteenth century Oromo pastoralists expanded into territories depopulated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-adal-war">Ethiopian–Adal War</a>. Their migration into the lowlands cut the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate</a> off from its pastoral hinterland and contributed decisively to its decline.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The mutual exhaustion produced by the war created the conditions for the Oromo migrations that permanently transformed the demographic and political geography of the region, as <b>Oromo</b> pastoralists expanded into territories depopulated by decades of conflict.</blockquote><a href="/empire/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In the interior, the expansion of the <b>Oromo</b> peoples into parts of southern Somalia in the sixteenth century and the rise of rival Somali clans placed growing pressure on Ajuran authority.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ajuran-sultanate">Ajuran Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>By 1978 Amhara officials ran the government in higher proportion than ever, and Amharas were resettled in the south to staff courts, churches and schools, where <b>Oromo</b> texts were replaced by Amharic.</blockquote><a href="/empire/derg">Derg · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Some languages once written in it, such as <b>Oromo</b>, have switched to Latin-based alphabets.</blockquote><a href="/language/geez">Ge'ez · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Joined by Julius and Kifla Krestos, Emperor Susenyos I of Ethiopia defeated <b>Oromo</b> raiders who had entered Begemder.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-17">17 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The name fell out of use as the area became part of <b>Oromo</b> Shewa.</blockquote><a href="/state/fatagar-sultanate">Fatagar Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
